Z||000000||1FST2M6||01-28-2001||11:36 AM||||ok i'm cruzin' up I-75 @70-75mph and made it one full tank (220mi-ish) then i get gas and i'm going about 80miles or so with the cruz set the car BACKFIRES THROUGH THE INTAKE!!!! shoot flames out the side of the car my 'rents FREEK out and thnk i'm dead I think i'm dead and the car well it shuts off and the trannys flaking (auto not under power still in gear) (i'm 300miles from home)so i stop and check everything and see that the lead on to the new coil was not tight (Crane PS91) i tighten it and the car was fine UNTIL i set the cruz again and it bucks and chokes and BACKFIRES THROUGH THE INTAKE!!  ok WHAT THE HELL!  the car is FINE as long as i DON"T set the cruise. WHAT is going on???? I can repeat it at anything over 2800rpm but not above 4500rpm (100mph+) what is going on?!?!?!?!  it's not bad gas cause i've gone through 6 other tank fulls and it STILL does it!  WTF!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig1fst.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||01-28-2001||01:04 PM||||The cruise control uses engine vacuum to actuate a diaphragm that is connected to a cable, which moves the throttle. You may have a vacuum leak in the vacuum circuit causing the fuel mixture to go lean and thus causing the bucking and popping. A tear in the diaphragm or a loose vacuum line is probably causing the vacuum leak somewhere in the circuit. The reason why it dose not happen as much when the engine rpm gets higher is because the effects of a vacuum leak grow less as the engine draws more air through the throttle body with higher rpm’s. The percent of air drawn though the vacuum leak get smaller as more air is drawn thought the throttle body as engine rpm climbs higher.<P>The reason why it does not happen to the engine when the cruise control is not on is because there is no engine vacuum applied to the diaphragm with the cruise control off thus bypassing the vacuum leak. Usually though bucking and popping through the intake system is associated with an ignition leak. Lean mixtures put the ignition system under more load making them work harder. <P><BR>Just my guess! <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-28-2001||03:18 PM||||That's a good first guess....<P>You need to check all the vac lines, the vac reserve can, and the cruise unit. Sounds an awfull lot like the cruise servo is toast but you still have to check all of it.||||reg||1
